# Rank 1595 / CVE-2023-44270: PostCSS's tokenizer recognised every
# line terminator except a bare carriage return in its bad-bracket
# pattern, so input using \r line endings kept an unclosed bracket
# token open across lines and confused every tool relying on
# PostCSS's token positions (incorrect parsing enabling downstream
# bypasses, CWE-74/CWE-144 family; the advisory names the security
# tools that sanitize CSS through PostCSS). RE_BAD_BRACKET in
# lib/tokenize.js read /.[\n"'(/\\]/ -- \n but not \r -- while the
# sibling terminator classes RE_AT_END and RE_WORD_END enumerate the
# full CSS whitespace set [\t\n\f\r ...] and the tokenizer's own
# whitespace case treats CR as a line terminator. Pool commit
# 58cc860b4c is the fix (COMMIT_OK -- "Fix carrier return
# parsing"): the class gains \r, /.[\r\n"'(/\\]/, with parse tests
# for \r-terminated input.
#
# ATTENTION, terminator-class completeness, held on recheck. The
# parser exists and its terminator handling is mostly right -- one
# character class omitted a terminator the same unit's own other
# enumerations carry, the grammar-completeness family of ranks 1573
# (single-quote encoding declarations), 1522, 1551 and 1564 -- and
# the evidence is internal to the file, unlike 1573's external XML
# grammar. Measured at the span tip on the real checkout (3 x
# --cache off per revision, timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB): both
# revisions report 14 findings, 7 at or above high, byte-identical
# finding sets, and -flags only reports one prototype-pollution flag
# in lib/parser.js at both revisions; vyql match on tokenize.js
# labels only the exported tokenizer's input parameter
# (code.ExternalEntryInput). No rule fires on the terminator class,
# and none could: the shipped JavaScript rule family for a regex
# that leaves line terminators unhandled is VYQL-INJ-056
# (code.IncompleteCommandEscapeRegex), whose binding is scoped to
# substitution escapers -- it requires a replace call with a
# backreference replacement, and a detector class fed to .test() has
# neither, which its own comment records as a deliberate exclusion
# of lexer and tokenizer regexes. The property a rule would need is
# per-literal -- "this character class enumerates a line terminator
# set that omits a member the same unit's other enumerations carry"
# -- and the binding vocabulary only offers substring tests over
# compacted regex token text, measured in three forms on the real
# pair: the module-wide negation the shipped binding uses is
# suppressed at the vulnerable revision too (the sibling classes
# mention \r, so "no regex mentions a terminator" is false); the
# co-membership form (a class with \n and a regex with \r in one
# module) fires at BOTH revisions because the fix only edits one of
# the three classes; and the positional needle that does separate
# the pair ([\n, the class opening with the line feed) encodes the
# fix's own layout -- the equally correct [\n\r"'(/\\] spelling
# still matches it -- and labels 23 further files across 10640
# collateral JavaScript files, many of them on fully-correct
# [\n\r\t] classes, 1 real in 24. No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: the
# missing piece is class-completeness vocabulary, not a localised
# construct. No definition change shipped and no engine commit.
# Spec cve_rank1595 records the \n-only bad-bracket class and the
# \r-complete form modelled on the real file, both asserting reject
# VYQL-INJ-056: when class-completeness vocabulary lands, the
# incomplete class must flip to expect.

test "rank1595: the bad bracket class omits the carriage return"
  lang javascript
  file lib/tokenize.js
  reject VYQL-INJ-056
  code
  ```
  'use strict'

  const SINGLE_QUOTE = "'".charCodeAt(0)
  const DOUBLE_QUOTE = '"'.charCodeAt(0)
  const BACKSLASH = '\\'.charCodeAt(0)
  const NEWLINE = '\n'.charCodeAt(0)
  const SPACE = ' '.charCodeAt(0)
  const FEED = '\f'.charCodeAt(0)
  const TAB = '\t'.charCodeAt(0)
  const CR = '\r'.charCodeAt(0)
  const OPEN_PARENTHESES = '('.charCodeAt(0)

  const RE_AT_END = /[\t\n\f\r "#'()/;[\\\]{}]/g
  const RE_WORD_END = /[\t\n\f\r !"#'():;@[\\\]{}]|\/(?=\*)/g
  const RE_BAD_BRACKET = /.[\n"'(/\\]/
  const RE_HEX_ESCAPE = /[\da-f]/i

  module.exports = function tokenizer(input, options = {}) {
    let css = input.css.valueOf()
    let ignore = options.ignoreErrors

    let code, next, content
    let length = css.length
    let pos = 0
    let buffer = []
    let currentToken

    function nextToken() {
      if (pos >= length) return
      code = css.charCodeAt(pos)

      switch (code) {
        case NEWLINE:
        case SPACE:
        case TAB:
        case CR:
        case FEED: {
          next = pos
          do {
            next += 1
            code = css.charCodeAt(next)
          } while (
            code === SPACE ||
            code === NEWLINE ||
            code === TAB ||
            code === CR ||
            code === FEED
          )

          currentToken = ['space', css.slice(pos, next)]
          pos = next - 1
          break
        }

        case OPEN_PARENTHESES: {
          code = css.charCodeAt(pos + 1)
          next = css.indexOf(')', pos + 1)
          content = css.slice(pos, next + 1)

          if (next === -1 || RE_BAD_BRACKET.test(content)) {
            currentToken = ['(', '(', pos]
          } else {
            currentToken = ['brackets', content, pos, next]
            pos = next
          }
          break
        }

        default:
          pos++
      }

      pos++
      return currentToken
    }

    return { nextToken, endOfFile: () => pos >= length }
  }
  ```

